# Copy this file to .env and fill in your key.
# Get a key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=AI...

# Optional: override the model used for content brainstorming/drafting.
# Defaults to the gemini-flash-latest alias, which has a low free-tier daily
# quota (20 requests/day as of writing). gemini-flash-lite-latest (the
# rolling alias for the current lite model) gives a much roomier free tier
# at the cost of some quality.
GEMINI_CONTENT_MODEL=gemini-flash-lite-latest

# The model that writes the words on a reel. Shown one still frame from the
# middle of each chosen shot — not the video — so it needs to be able to see
# images but not to understand motion. Falls back to GEMINI_CONTENT_MODEL.
#
# Captions are the only part of a reel build that asks a model anything, and
# the only part allowed to fail without failing the build: no key, a 429, or a
# malformed answer all produce a reel with no text on it and a note saying so.
# On a free-tier key that is a routine event rather than an edge case.
GEMINI_REEL_MODEL=gemini-flash-lite-latest

# Business backlog / sprint board (GitHub Projects v2). Needs a classic PAT
# with the "repo" and "project" scopes (read:project alone can read the
# board but can't update Status/Assignee or create the "Assigned To" field).
GITHUB_TOKEN=ghp_...
GITHUB_OWNER=your-github-org-or-user
GITHUB_PROJECT_NUMBER=1
# Repo that backlog draft cards get converted into real Issues in.
GITHUB_REPO=

# Weekend Prep Planner "Fetch from Odoo" — pulls confirmed, B2C (Individual
# contact type) Sales Orders in a date range straight into Step 1, instead of
# entering them by hand. Generate an API key in Odoo under
# Settings > Users & Companies > Users > (your user) > Account Security > API Keys.
ODOO_URL=https://yourcompany.odoo.com
ODOO_DB=yourcompany
ODOO_USERNAME=you@yourcompany.com
ODOO_API_KEY=

# Ops Dashboard "Service weeks" strip — which weeks the kitchen is open for,
# read/written straight on Odoo's custom Service Weeks model. All four are
# OPTIONAL: the server discovers the model and its From / To / Kitchen fields
# by introspecting Odoo on first use, because a Studio model's technical names
# differ per database. Only fill these in if discovery picks the wrong field —
# GET /api/ops/service-weeks/schema shows what it resolved.
ODOO_SERVICE_WEEK_MODEL=
ODOO_SERVICE_WEEK_FROM_FIELD=
ODOO_SERVICE_WEEK_TO_FIELD=
ODOO_SERVICE_WEEK_STATUS_FIELD=

# Order Packing board — the sale.order selection field the per-order
# Fulfilment Status dropdown writes to (IN_SMOKER / PREPPING / PACKED /
# PARTNER_ASGN / OUT_FOR_DEL / DELIVERED / INVOICED). OPTIONAL for the same
# reason as the Service Weeks fields above: it's a Studio field, so the server
# discovers it by looking for a selection field named/labelled "Fulfilment
# Status". Only set this if it picks the wrong one (typically x_fulfilment_status).
ODOO_FULFILMENT_FIELD=

# The SQLite database everything ops-side reads and writes: the catalogue and
# menu, purchases and stock, smoking sessions, the weekend prep board, the
# order pipeline and the B2B client book. It was seeded once from the sibling
# knowledge-base repo's CSVs and has been the source of truth since; nothing
# under server/ reads a CSV any more, so that repo no longer has to be checked
# out for the app to run.
#
# Defaults to server/data/smokerings.db inside this project — gitignored, like
# server/logs and server/uploads, so take your own backups of it. Build an
# empty one with `npm run db:init`; only set this to keep the database
# somewhere else entirely.
# KB_SQLITE_PATH=D:\Personal GIT\SmokeRingsBBQ Automation\server\data\smokerings.db

# Where Daily View's two CSV mirrors are written. OPTIONAL, and only a
# destination — nothing under server/ reads a CSV, so the app runs identically
# with this unset and that repo not checked out at all.
#
# Daily View writes its cadence and its per-week tick log to the database, and
# then writes the same two tables out as
#   <this>/Tasks/schedule.csv
#   <this>/Tasks/weekly_schedule_status_log.csv
# so both stay readable in a spreadsheet. The database is always the source of
# truth: editing either file changes nothing and the next save overwrites it.
# A mirror that can't be written (repo not checked out, file open in Excel)
# is logged and never fails the save.
#
# Defaults to the sibling knowledge-base repo's Data directory, which is where
# these two files already live. `npm run csv:mirror` rewrites both on demand —
# after a `npm run db --` one-liner, say, which changes the table without
# going through the app.
# KNOWLEDGE_BASE_DATA_DIR=D:\Personal GIT\knowledge-base\Data

# Optional: pins the Odoo product category holding the B2B wholesale products,
# by id or name fragment. The menu editor splits B2C from B2B on this category
# (see server/ops/menu/menuItems.js); it is discovered by name ("b2b") when unset.
ODOO_B2B_MENU_CATEGORY=

# Marketing ROI — the "Order Source" field revenue is grouped by. OPTIONAL,
# for the same reason as ODOO_FULFILMENT_FIELD above: it is a Studio selection
# (Instagram / Reddit / WhatsApp / Website / Swiggy / Zomato / Friends &
# Family / B2B / Catering / Pop-up Event), so the server discovers it by
# looking for a selection field named or labelled "Order Source". Only set
# this if discovery picks the wrong one (typically x_order_source).
#
# Note that the channel list itself is NEVER configured here — it is read live
# out of Odoo's own selection, so adding a channel in Odoo Studio makes it
# available on the ROI screen the same day. What each channel maps onto in
# UTM and Google Analytics terms is the CHANNELS table at the top of
# server/marketing/orderAttribution.js.
ODOO_ORDER_SOURCE_FIELD=

# Marketing ROI — the traffic half of the screen (sessions per source and
# campaign, and the conversion rate that comes with it). BOTH are optional:
# with neither set, the screen says Google Analytics is not connected and
# reports invested vs generated from Odoo and the spend ledger alone, which is
# the half that answers "did this pay".
#
# Set-up, once:
#   1. Google Cloud console > IAM & Admin > Service Accounts > create one,
#      then Keys > Add key > JSON. It downloads once — copy the two fields
#      below out of it, then the file is not needed again.
#   2. On that Cloud project, enable the "Google Analytics Data API".
#   3. In GA4: Admin > Property Access Management > add the service account's
#      client_email as a Viewer on the property.
#   4. Fill in the three below and restart the server.
#
# GA4_PROPERTY_ID is the numeric property id (Admin > Property Settings), NOT
# the G-XXXXXXX measurement id.
GA4_PROPERTY_ID=

# The "client_email" field of the service account JSON. Looks like
# something@your-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=

# The "private_key" field of the same JSON — the whole block including the
# BEGIN/END lines. Two ways to write it, both fine:
#   one line, keeping the \n escapes exactly as the JSON has them:
#     GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIIE...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"
#   or across real lines, in double quotes:
#     GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----
#     MIIE...
#     -----END PRIVATE KEY-----"
# NOT the "private_key_id" — that 40-character hex string is an identifier,
# not a credential, and cannot be turned back into one.
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Alternatives to the two fields above, for hosts that hand secrets over
# whole. Set ONE of these instead, not alongside:
#   GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY       the entire JSON, raw or base64-encoded
#   GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY_FILE  a path to the downloaded JSON file
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY=
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY_FILE=

# Optional: what the QR & Link Builder pre-fills the destination box with
# (Marketing > QR & Link Builder). Only a starting value — every link built
# there can point anywhere, and with this unset the box simply opens empty.
# Set it to whatever you most often send people to: the order page, not the
# home page, since a tracked link is worth the most where it converts.
MARKETING_SITE_URL=https://smokerings.in/order

# Customer Map (Marketing > Customer Map) — OPTIONAL, and the screen works
# with it unset.
#
# Customer addresses live in Odoo as free text; the map needs coordinates, so
# the first time an address is seen it is looked up at OpenStreetMap's
# Nominatim and the answer is kept in the local geocode_cache table forever.
# No key and no account: what Nominatim asks for instead is that requests
# identify themselves and come no faster than one a second
# (https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/nominatim/). Both are already
# honoured; this only puts a real contact address in the User-Agent, which is
# how they reach you before blocking anybody, and makes a run less likely to
# be rate-limited.
#
# WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU USE THE SCREEN: pressing "Put these on the map"
# sends those customer addresses to a third party. That is why it is a button
# rather than something a page load does, why every answer is cached so the
# same address is never sent twice, and why an address that cannot be found
# gets a pin dropped by hand instead of being sent again.
GEOCODER_EMAIL=

# --- Insta Reel Generator (Marketing > Insta Reel Generator) ---------------
#
# The editor and the export need nothing configured: ffmpeg ships with the
# npm install (ffmpeg-static), so uploading clips, trimming them, burning
# captions and downloading the finished 1080x1920 mp4 all work out of the box.
# Everything below is only for the last step — posting it to Instagram from
# the screen instead of from your phone.
#
# The thing to understand first, because it drives the whole setup: the
# Instagram Graph API does not accept a file upload. You give Meta a URL and
# Meta's own servers come and fetch the video. That means this server has to
# be reachable from the public internet over HTTPS for the length of a
# publish, which is what PUBLIC_BASE_URL is.
#
# THERE ARE TWO OF THESE APIS AND THEY ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE. Meta ships
# "Instagram API with Facebook Login" (graph.facebook.com, tokens start EAA...,
# needs a linked Facebook Page) and "Instagram API with Instagram Login"
# (graph.instagram.com, tokens start IGAA..., no Facebook Page anywhere). The
# endpoints and the three-step publish are identical; the hosts are not, and
# each rejects the other's token with "Invalid OAuth access token - Cannot
# parse access token", which names neither the cause nor the cure.
#
# You do not have to say which you are using. server/marketing/instagramGraph.js
# picks the host off the token's prefix. IG_API_HOST overrides it if Meta ever
# renames a host. THIS SETUP USES INSTAGRAM LOGIN — the steps below describe
# the Facebook Login route, which is the longer of the two; for Instagram
# Login it is just "add Instagram Login to an app, authorise the account, and
# exchange the token for a long-lived one at
# graph.instagram.com/access_token?grant_type=ig_exchange_token".
#
# Long-lived Instagram Login tokens last 60 days and are refreshable in place,
# before they expire, with no user interaction:
#   https://graph.instagram.com/refresh_access_token?grant_type=ig_refresh_token
#     &access_token=CURRENT_TOKEN
# Every refresh resets the clock to 60 days. Let one lapse and somebody has to
# sign in again.
#
# Set-up, once (the Facebook Login route):
#   1. The Instagram account must be a Business or Creator account, and it
#      must be linked to a Facebook Page (Instagram app > Settings > Account
#      type and tools). A personal account cannot publish through the API at
#      all, and this is the step that most often turns out to be the problem.
#   2. developers.facebook.com > My Apps > Create App > Business. Add the
#      "Instagram Graph API" product and link the Page from step 1.
#   3. Graph API Explorer: pick the app, pick the Page, and request the
#      permissions instagram_basic, instagram_content_publish and
#      pages_show_list. Generate a user token.
#   4. That token lasts an hour. Exchange it for a long-lived one (about 60
#      days) and put THAT in IG_ACCESS_TOKEN:
#        https://graph.facebook.com/v23.0/oauth/access_token
#          ?grant_type=fb_exchange_token&client_id=APP_ID
#          &client_secret=APP_SECRET&fb_exchange_token=SHORT_TOKEN
#      It expires. When posting starts failing with an auth error, this is
#      why, and step 4 is the fix.
#   5. Get the Instagram user id (NOT the @handle, and not the Page id):
#        https://graph.facebook.com/v23.0/me/accounts?access_token=TOKEN
#        https://graph.facebook.com/v23.0/PAGE_ID
#          ?fields=instagram_business_account&access_token=TOKEN
#   6. Set PUBLIC_BASE_URL (below), restart the server, and use "Check the
#      connection" on the screen — it answers with the @handle it is about to
#      post as, which is worth doing before the first real post.
IG_ACCESS_TOKEN=
IG_USER_ID=

# Only needed to refresh an Instagram Login token; not used to publish.
IG_APP_ID=
IG_APP_SECRET=

# Forces which API host is used, e.g. graph.instagram.com. Leave blank — the
# token's own prefix decides, and that is right in every case seen so far.
IG_API_HOST=

# How the outside world reaches this server, with no trailing slash. Instagram
# fetches the rendered video from PUBLIC_BASE_URL/api/marketing/reel/
# render-file/... so this must be public HTTPS: localhost, 192.168.x.x and a
# plain-http URL are all rejected before a publish is attempted, because Meta
# would only answer with an unexplainable "media could not be fetched".
#
# On this setup that is Tailscale Funnel, which unlike `tailscale serve` is
# reachable from outside the tailnet:
#     tailscale funnel 4000
# then use the https://<machine>.<tailnet>.ts.net URL it prints. Funnel only
# needs to be up while a publish is running.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# Optional. Meta supports each Graph API version for about two years; bump
# this when a version is retired rather than editing
# server/marketing/instagramGraph.js.
IG_GRAPH_VERSION=

# Optional. The .ttf used for captions burnt into the video. Unset, the first
# of Arial Bold / Segoe UI Bold / Arial that exists on the machine is used;
# set it to point at a brand font instead. Only consulted when a clip
# actually has a caption on it.
REEL_FONT_FILE=

# Optional. Override the bundled ffmpeg/ffprobe with a system install — for a
# hardware-accelerated build, say. Unset, the binaries from the ffmpeg-static
# and ffprobe-static packages are used, which is what makes the export work
# with nothing installed.
FFMPEG_PATH=
FFPROBE_PATH=

# ---- Reel Studio: YouTube -------------------------------------------------
# The Share step's YouTube tile. Uploads the rendered file straight from this
# machine, so unlike Instagram it needs no tunnel and no PUBLIC_BASE_URL.
#
# This is the one Google credential that is NOT the service account, and not
# by oversight: the YouTube Data API refuses service accounts outright,
# because a video has to be owned by a channel and a service account has no
# channel. So a human signs in once and the refresh token that leaves behind
# is what gets used from then on.
#
#   1. Google Cloud console > APIs & Services > Library > enable
#      "YouTube Data API v3". Use the same project the service account is on.
#   2. OAuth consent screen > External. Add your own Google account as a test
#      user. THEN PUBLISH IT — a consent screen left in "Testing" makes Google
#      expire the refresh token every seven days, and the upload starts
#      failing with invalid_grant a week after it last worked.
#   3. Credentials > Create credentials > OAuth client ID > Desktop app.
#      Paste the two values below.
#   4. npm run youtube:login — signs in and prints the refresh token to paste.
#
# TWO LIMITS WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU RELY ON THIS:
#
#   * An API project that has not passed YouTube's compliance audit has every
#     upload forced to PRIVATE, whatever visibility is asked for, and cannot
#     make it public through the API either. The video is real and it is in
#     Studio; somebody publishes it by hand. The Share step says so when it
#     happens rather than claiming the video is live. Applying for the audit
#     is what lifts it.
#   * Uploads cost 1600 of a project's 10,000 daily quota units — about six
#     videos a day, across everything on the project — resetting at midnight
#     US Pacific.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# Drive's separate sign-in — see the Drive block below for why it cannot
# share the one above. Minted by `npm run drive:login`.
GOOGLE_DRIVE_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# ---- Reel Studio: Google Drive --------------------------------------------
# One folder does two jobs now, and they pull in opposite directions.
#
#   READING. It is where a reel is made from. The clips shot during a cook go
#   into it from a phone, along with whatever songs are worth laying under
#   them, and the Insta Reel Generator lists exactly that folder and nothing
#   else. Nothing is uploaded through the dashboard.
#
#   WRITING. It is also where the finished master lands. Instagram and YouTube
#   both re-encode and neither hands the original back, so the Share step's
#   Drive tile is the untouched copy.
#
# Auth is Drive's own OAuth sign-in, NOT the GA4 service account. A service
# account has no Drive storage of its own, so any file it creates is owned by
# a zero-byte identity and the upload dies with "The user's Drive storage
# quota has been exceeded" — which reads like a full disk and is nothing of
# the sort. Google's fix is to put the folder in a Shared Drive, a Workspace
# feature a free Gmail account cannot create. Signing in as the person
# sidesteps it: the files are owned by the account that owns the Drive, on its
# own 15GB.
#
# TWO SCOPES, AND WHY BOTH ARE NEEDED:
#
#   drive.file      Per-file access to files this app itself created. This is
#                   what uploads the finished reels and keeps owning them.
#
#   drive.readonly  Read access, which is the only way to see the source
#                   footage. A clip dropped into the folder from a phone was
#                   not created by this app, so drive.file cannot see it — the
#                   listing comes back empty however full the folder is.
#
# Google allows both in one consent screen because both are Drive. (The
# YouTube pairing below is the one it refuses.)
#
# "ONLY THAT FOLDER" IS ENFORCED BY THE CODE, NOT BY THE TOKEN. Google sells
# no folder-scoped Drive scope. drive.readonly can read the whole account, and
# what keeps this app inside one folder is that every read in
# server/integrations/googleDrive.js pins GOOGLE_DRIVE_FOLDER_ID as a parent,
# and every import re-checks the file is really in it before downloading a
# byte. There is deliberately no way to ask a route for a different folder.
# server/integrations/driveLibrary.test.js is what holds that claim up.
#
# It is read-only rather than the full `drive` scope on purpose: the source
# folder is only ever read, so nothing here can rewrite or delete something
# you put in it. Writing is confined to drive.file, which reaches this app's
# own renders and nothing else.
#
# Set-up, once:
#   1. Enable "Google Drive API" on the same Cloud project as the OAuth client.
#   2. npm run drive:login — Drive's OWN sign-in, separate from YouTube's.
#      Google rejects a consent request that asks for youtube.upload alongside
#      another API's scope ("contains scopes that cannot be requested
#      together"), so the two cannot share one authorisation and each stores
#      its own refresh token. Being signed in to YouTube does nothing here.
#      Paste what it prints into GOOGLE_DRIVE_REFRESH_TOKEN, above.
#   3. npm run drive:setup — creates the folder and prints its id to paste
#      below. Safe to re-run; it finds the folder it made rather than making
#      a second one.
#
#      Or paste the id of a folder you already have, straight out of the
#      address bar — the URL form is accepted and trimmed. That works now that
#      the app can read folders it did not create, and it is the better option
#      if the phone already syncs footage somewhere.
#
# IF THE LISTING IS EMPTY BUT THE FOLDER IS NOT, the refresh token predates
# the source library and carries only drive.file. Re-run `npm run drive:login`.
#
# The folder is yours: move it, rename it, share it. Only the id is used here,
# and that survives all three.
GOOGLE_DRIVE_FOLDER_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PUSH NOTIFICATIONS — reminders for what is still pending today
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The dashboard sits on the phone as a Chrome home-screen shortcut. These
# keys are what let the server push a reminder to it when nobody has the
# dashboard open.
#
# SETUP
#   1. npm run push:keys — prints a VAPID key pair. Paste both below.
#   2. Restart `node server/index.js` (it reads these once, at startup).
#   3. On the phone, open Daily View and turn on "Remind me on this device".
#      That grants notification permission and registers the subscription.
#
# WHY IT DOES NOT NEED A PWA INSTALL. Notification permission is granted per
# origin, not per installed app, so the plain home-screen shortcut receives
# these exactly as an installed PWA would. What it does need is an https://
# origin — which the tailnet URL already is, under a real certificate that
# Tailscale serves. Opening the dashboard on a bare LAN IP over http will
# not work: the browser hides the service worker API entirely there.
#
# WHAT THIS CANNOT DO. The notification travels from this machine, through
# the browser vendor's push service, to the handset — so it arrives off the
# tailnet, on mobile data, anywhere. But legion has to be awake with the API
# server running for anything to be sent at all. A reminder missed because
# the machine was asleep is not delivered late; it is not delivered.
#
# REGENERATING THE KEYS invalidates every subscription at once — each phone
# has to turn notifications off and on again. `npm run push:keys` refuses to
# overwrite an existing pair unless passed --force, for that reason.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# A contact address the push service can use if this server misbehaves.
# Must be a mailto: or https: URL. Defaults to the team address if unset.
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:smokerings.team@gmail.com

# When the morning digest goes out, as 24h local time. Everything still open
# for today arrives in one notification at this hour. Per-task nudges are
# separate and fire at each task's own time_of_day, whatever this says.
# Default 07:00.
PUSH_DIGEST_TIME=07:00

# Set to `off` to stop the reminder timer entirely, without unsubscribing
# any phone — the toggle stays on and nothing is sent. Useful on a second
# checkout of this repo, which would otherwise send a duplicate of every
# reminder the real server is already sending.
#PUSH_REMINDERS=off
